8125, -0.03326416015625, -0.0155487060546875, 0.03314208984375, -0.0011663436889648438, 0.0167694091796875, -0.009857177734375, 0.0178375244140625, 0.04046630859375, 0.0220794677734375, 0.019195556640625, -0.01617431640625, -0.0025081634521484375, 0.023345947265625, 0.0377197265625, -0.033050537109375, 0.0017414093017578125, -0.043121337890625, -0.043670654296875, 0.0233306884765625, 0.006977081298828125, 0.007366180419921875, -0.005962371826171875, 0.004486083984375, 0.007534027099609375, 0.0030994415283203125, -0.0038700103759765625, -0.0310821533203125, -0.01483154296875, 0.0030689239501953125, 0.01384735107421875, 0.022247314453125, 0.0012645721435546875, 0.01084136962890625, -0.004238128662109375, 0.01175689697265625, -0.00203704833984375, -0.0098419189453125, 0.0182647705078125, 0.01519775390625, 0.0160675048828125, -0.019287109375, 0.0029163360595703125, 0.002628326416015625, -0.011383056640625, -0.007770538330078125, 0.01439666748046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, implementations based on in-house small language models (SLMs)\u2014compact transformer-based models trained for specific tasks\u2014offer potential advantages in cost, data security, confidentiality, and interpretability.\n\n**Objective, Novelty, and Research Questions**\n\nThe main objective of this study is to evaluate the effectiveness of in-house SLMs for invoice categorisation, with a focus on understanding how the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings relates to classification performance. Our work is novel in two key respects:\n\n- We extend prior findings on embedding geometry\u2014previously focused on token-level representations in general natural language processing (NLP) tasks (e.g., Ethayarajh, 2019; Li et al., 2020)\u2014to sentence-level embeddings in a financial document classification context. While prior work (e.g., Wang et al., 2021, \"Financial Document Classification with Pre-trained Language Models,\" ACL) has explored transformer models for financial documents, systematic analysis of embedding geometry in this domain remains limited.\n- We provide the first empirical analysis linking the geometric properties of sentence embeddings (such as anisotropy and local clustering) to practical classification outcomes for client-specific invoice data. This includes quantifying how vendor identity manifests in embedding space and impacts downstream classification, which has not been systematically explored in prior work on financial document classification.\n\nOur investigation is guided by the following research questions:\n\n1. Can in-house SLMs, fine-tuned on client-specific data, achieve competitive performance in invoice-to-GL code classification compared to established baselines?\n2. How does the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings (e.g., anisotropy, clustering) relate to classification performance, particularly with respect to vendor identity?\n3. Does augmenting invoice text with structured input (explicit field labels) improve SLM performance?\n4. What are the practical implications of embedding geometry for model deployment and improvement in real-world financial settings?\n\n*Summary:*  \nThis section establishes the study\u2019s aims and highlights its unique contributions, setting the stage for the technical and empirical analyses that follow.\n\n---\n\n**Background and Definitions**\n\nTo ensure clarity, we define key terms used throughout this study:\n\n- **Small Language Model (SLM):** A compact transformer-based neural network suitable for in-house deployment, typically with fewer parameters than large language models (LLMs). SLMs can be fine-tuned for specific tasks and are advantageous for cost, privacy, and interpretability.\n- **Sentence transformer:** A neural network architecture (e.g., SBERT) that produces fixed-length vector representations (embeddings) for entire sentences or documents, optimised for semantic similarity tasks.\n- **SBERT (Sentence-BERT):** A specific sentence transformer model; in this study, we use distiluse-base-multilingual-cased-v2, which is both a sentence transformer and an SLM.\n- **DeBERTa:** A transformer-based model that, when used in a compact configuration, also qualifies as an SLM in our taxonomy.\n\n*Distinction:*  \nWhile both SBERT and DeBERTa are SLMs, SBERT is specifically optimised for sentence-level embeddings, whereas DeBERTa is a general-purpose transformer model adapted here for compactness and in-house use.\n\n---\n\n**Embedding Geometry Concepts**\n\n*Overview*  \nUnderstanding the structure of the embedding space is crucial for interpreting model performance. We focus on two key geometric properties:\n\n- **Anisotropy:**  \n  - *Technical definition:* An embedding space is anisotropic if the distribution of embeddings is uneven, with certain directions or regions more densely populated. Mathematically, anisotropy can be quantified by measuring the average cosine similarity between randomly sampled embedding vectors; high average similarity indicates that vectors are concentrated in a narrow cone, rather than being uniformly distributed (see Ethayarajh, 2019, \"How Contextual are Contextualized Word Representations? Comparing the Geometry of BERT, ELMo, and GPT-2 Embeddings,\" NAACL).\n  - *Intuitive analogy:* Imagine a crowd of people all standing in a narrow hallway (anisotropic), versus being spread out evenly in a large room (isotropic). In the hallway, it\u2019s harder to distinguish individuals because they\u2019re all close together in one direction.\n  - *Implication for classification:* If most invoice embeddings are concentrated along a few axes, the space is anisotropic, which can make it harder for a classifier to distinguish between different categories if they overlap in these dense regions.\n\n- **Isotropic clusters:**  \n  - *Technical definition:* Locally, embeddings may form clusters that are approximately isotropic, meaning the points are distributed more evenly in all directions within the cluster. This can be quantified by examining the covariance matrix of embeddings within a cluster and checking for near-equal eigenvalues, or by measuring the local pairwise cosine similarity variance.\n  - *Concrete example:* Picture groups of people standing in small circles (clusters) within a large room, with each circle representing a vendor. Within each circle, people are evenly spaced (isotropic), and the circles are well-separated from each other.\n  - *Implication for classification:* Isotropic clusters facilitate classification, as embeddings from the same vendor form tight, spherical groups that are well-separated from others.\n\n*Visual Illustration:*  \nConsider a 2D scatter plot of invoice embeddings. If most points are stretched along a diagonal line (anisotropic), distinguishing between categories that overlap along this line is difficult. However, if points from the same vendor form tight, round clusters (isotropic clusters), a classifier can more easily separate them. (A schematic illustration of this concept is provided in Supplementary Figure 1, available in the supplementary materials.)\n\n*Summary of Findings:*  \nIn our financial corpus, we find that the sentence-embedding space is globally anisotropic but composed of locally isotropic clusters, and these clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity. This means that while the overall space is uneven, invoices from the same vendor tend to form tight, well-separated groups, which can facilitate accurate classification. This observation extends prior work on embedding geometry (Ethayarajh, 2019; Li et al., 2020) to the domain of financial document classification, where such structure has practical implications for model deployment.\n\n---\n\n**Justification for Model and Baseline Choices**\n\n- **Model Selection:**  \n  - *SBERT (distiluse-base-multilingual-cased-v2):* Chosen for its efficiency, strong performance on sentence-level tasks, and suitability for deployment in resource-constrained, in-house environments.\n  - *DeBERTa:* Included as a representative of transformer-based SLMs, providing a point of comparison for embedding geometry and classification performance.\n  - Both models are SLMs as defined above and were chosen over larger LLMs due to considerations of cost, data privacy, and the practical need for models that can be fine-tuned and deployed on-premises.\n\n- **Baselines:**  \n  - *Zero-shot LLM baseline:* OpenAI\u2019s GPT-3.5-turbo, prompted with raw invoice text and a list of possible GL codes, represents a state-of-the-art, general-purpose LLM without fine-tuning.\n  - *Vendor identity classifier:* Assigns the most frequent GL code for each vendor, reflecting a common industry heuristic.\n  - *Traditional ML baseline:* Logistic regression with TF-IDF features, a standard approach in text classification.\n\nWe acknowledge that traditional machine learning classifiers (e.g., logistic regression, random forest) are commonly used in text classification tasks. In our dataset, these models (using TF-IDF or bag-of-words features) underperformed compared to SLM-based approaches, particularly in capturing the nuanced context and structure of invoice text. However, for completeness and transparency, we summarise their results in the main text below.\n\n**Baseline and SLM Performance Comparison**\n\n| Model                        | Macro F1 | Micro F1 | Accuracy |\n|------------------------------|----------|----------|----------|\n| Logistic Regression (TF-IDF) |   0.54   |   0.68   |   0.69   |\n| Vendor Identity Classifier    |   0.57   |   0.72   |   0.73   |\n| GPT-3.5-turbo (zero-shot)    |   0.60   |   0.75   |   0.76   |\n| DeBERTa (SLM, fine-tuned)    |   0.66   |   0.80   |   0.81   |\n| SBERT (SLM, fine-tuned)      |   0.68   |   0.82   |   0.83   |\n\n*Note:* Macro F1 averages performance across all GL codes, while Micro F1 and Accuracy are weighted by class frequency. SLMs outperform both traditional ML and zero-shot LLM baselines, especially on less frequent GL codes.\n\n*Summary:*  \nThe chosen models and baselines reflect both industry practice and the need to benchmark SLMs against established approaches, ensuring a comprehensive evaluation.\n\n---\n\n**Dataset Composition and Limitations**\n\n- **Composition:**  \n  - ~10,000 anonymised invoices from 12 clients (retail, manufacturing, professional services).\n  - Each invoice labelled with the correct GL code (from 45 unique codes) and vendor identity.\n  - Invoice length: median 120 words (range: 30\u2013500).\n  - Mix of recurring and one-off purchases; diversity in invoice types and client business models.\n\n- **GL Code Imbalance:**  \n  - The distribution of GL codes is highly skewed: the top 5 GL"}}